Here's a recommended structure for testing your C# MCP server tools without hitting the real API:

## Project Structure

```
MyMcpServer/
├── src/
│   └── MyMcpServer/
│       ├── Tools/
│       │   └── MyTool.cs
│       ├── Services/
│       │   ├── IApiClient.cs        # Interface for HTTP calls
│       │   └── ApiClient.cs         # Real implementation
│       └── MyMcpServer.csproj
└── tests/
    └── MyMcpServer.Tests/
        ├── Tools/
        │   └── MyToolTests.cs
        ├── Fakes/
        │   └── FakeMessageHandler.cs  # Or use MockHttpMessageHandler
        └── MyMcpServer.Tests.csproj
```

## Key Patterns

**1. Abstract HTTP behind an interface:**

```csharp
public interface IApiClient
{
    Task<ApiResponse> GetResourceAsync(string id);
}
```

**2. Inject it into your tool class:**

```csharp
public class MyTool(IApiClient apiClient)
{
    public async Task<string> ExecuteAsync(string input)
    {
        var result = await apiClient.GetResourceAsync(input);
        return result.Data;
    }
}
```

**3. Mock in tests (using `NSubstitute` or `Moq`):**

```csharp
[Fact]
public async Task ExecuteAsync_ReturnsExpectedData()
{
    var fakeClient = Substitute.For<IApiClient>();
    fakeClient.GetResourceAsync("123")
        .Returns(new ApiResponse { Data = "test-value" });

    var tool = new MyTool(fakeClient);
    var result = await tool.ExecuteAsync("123");

    Assert.Equal("test-value", result);
}
```

**4. Alternatively, use `MockHttpMessageHandler` (from `RichardSzalay.MockHttp`) if you want to test closer to the HTTP layer:**

```csharp
var handler = new MockHttpMessageHandler();
handler.When("/api/resource/123")
       .Respond("application/json", """{"data":"test-value"}""");

var httpClient = new HttpClient(handler) { BaseAddress = new Uri("https://fake") };
var apiClient = new ApiClient(httpClient);
```

## Test project dependencies

```xml
<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.*" />
<PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.*" />
<PackageReference Include="NSubstitute" Version="5.*" />
<PackageReference Include="RichardSzalay.MockHttp" Version="7.*" />
```

## Summary

| Layer | Test Strategy |
|-------|--------------|
| Tool logic | Mock `IApiClient` |
| HTTP serialization | Use `MockHttpMessageHandler` |
| Integration | Use `WebApplicationFactory` or TestServer with fake handlers |

The critical design decision is **not** calling `HttpClient` directly in your tool classes — always go through an injectable abstraction so tests stay fast and deterministic.
